{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2090 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "f88f62126891489271f199dfb9f955f48467a84964509cbd171831c598d10686" :edb/db-before-t 2089 :edb/db-after-t 2090 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535402 50 #inst "2026-10-08T23:25:55.993Z" 13194139535402 true] [17592186175704 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2$, given by the function $a(t) = 3t^2+2t-6$, where $t > 0$ is the time in seconds. The positive direction is left to right. If the particle is stationary when $t=0\\,\\textrm s$, determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving to the right." 13194139535402 false] [17592186175704 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2,$ given by the function $a(t) = 3t^2+2t-6,$ where $t > 0$ is the time in seconds. The positive direction is left to right. If the particle is stationary when $t=0\\,\\textrm s,$ determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving to the right." 13194139535402 true] [17592186175704 1145 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ∫a(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫(3t^{2} + 2t - 6)dt \\\\\n&= t^{3} + t^{2} - 6t + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the particle is stationary at the time $t=0$. This means $v(0) = 0$, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 &= (0)^{3} + (0)^{2} - 6(0) + C \\\\\n0 &= C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore the velocity of the particle is\n\n$$\nv(t) = t^3+t^2-6t\n$$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *right*, we must solve the inequality $v > 0$. This gives\n\n$$\nt^3+t^2-6t = t(t+3)(t-2) > 0\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3582/Source/Images/8f7445db34aa0dcfa29db94a8442208a.png)\n\nThe particle moves to the right when $v(t)$ is positive. So, from the graph, we see that the particle moves to the right when $t>2$." 13194139535402 false] [17592186175704 1145 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}+2t-6)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-6t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the particle is stationary at the time $t=0.$ This means $v(0) = 0,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(0)^{3}+(0)^{2}-6(0)+C \\\\ 0 & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore the velocity of the particle is\n\n$$\nv(t) = t^3+t^2-6t.\n$$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *right*, we must solve the inequality $v > 0.$ This gives\n\n$$\nt^3+t^2-6t = t(t+3)(t-2) > 0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t),$ we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/assets/03b5ff6282d59038cbb355c0c3f017b544f286228ba4c3a82329897b4b43674c.png)\n\nThe particle moves to the right when $v(t)$ is positive. So, from the graph, we see that the particle moves to the right when $t>2.$" 13194139535402 true] [17592186175705 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2$, given by the function $a(t) = 6t-3t^2$, where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=1\\,\\textrm s$, calculate the maximum velocity $v_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity." 13194139535402 false] [17592186175705 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2,$ given by the function $a(t) = 6t-3t^2,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=1\\,\\textrm s,$ calculate the maximum velocity $v_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity." 13194139535402 true] [17592186175705 1145 "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v$.\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d t} = a(t)\n$$\n\nto find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t) = 0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(t) &= 0 \\\\\n6t - 3t^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n3t(2 - t) &= 0 \\\\\nt &= 0, 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t> 0$, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when\n\n$$\nt = 2\\,\\textrm s\n$$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv^{″}(t) &= \\frac{d^{2}v}{dt^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dv}{dt}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(a(t)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(6t - 3t^{2}) \\\\\n&= 6 - 6t \\\\\nv^{″}(2) &= 6 - 6(2) \\\\\n&=-6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v''(2) < 0$, we conclude that the velocity is maximized when\n\n$$\nt=2\\,\\textrm s\n$$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ∫a(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫(6t - 3t^{2})dt \\\\\n&= 3t^{2} - t^{3} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1) = 2$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(1)^{2} - (1)^{3} + C &= 2 \\\\\n3 - 1 + C &= 2⟹C = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nv(t) =3t^2-t^3\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\nv_{\\text{max}}= v(2) = 3(2)^2 - (2)^3 =4\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity\n\n$$\nv_{\\text{max}}=4\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s\n$$\n\nis reached at\n\n$$\nt=2\\,\\textrm s\n$$" 13194139535402 false] [17592186175705 1145 "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d t} = a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t) = 0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 6t-3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3t(2-t) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t> 0$, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t = 2\\,\\textrm s.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =6-6t \\\\ {v}^{″}(2) & =6-6(2) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v''(2) <      0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=2\\,\\textrm s.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (6t-3{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1) = 2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(1)^{2}-(1)^{3}+C & =2 \\\\ 3-1+C & =2\\,⟹\\,C=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) =3t^2-t^3.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\nv_{\\text{max}}= v(2) = 3(2)^2 - (2)^3 =4\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity $v_{\\text{max}}=4\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ is reached at $t=2\\,\\textrm s.$" 13194139535402 true] [17592186175722 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2$, given by the function $a(t) =2t-6$, where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=4\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=1\\,\\textrm s$, determine the moments in time when the particle is stationary." 13194139535402 false] [17592186175722 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2,$ given by the function $a(t) =2t-6$, where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=4\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=1\\,\\textrm s$, determine the moments in time when the particle is stationary." 13194139535402 true] [17592186175722 1145 "The particle is stationary when\n\n$$\nv=0\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ∫a(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫(2t - 6)dt \\\\\n&= t^{2} - 6t + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1) =4$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 &= (1)^{2} - 6(1) + C \\\\\n4 &= -5 + C \\\\\nC &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nv(t) = t^2-6t + 9\n$$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when\n\n$$\nv=0\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} - 6t + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n(t - 3)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nt &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when\n\n$$\nt=3 \\,\\textrm s\n$$" 13194139535402 false] [17592186175722 1145 "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t-6)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-6t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1) =4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =(1)^{2}-6(1)+C \\\\ 4 & =-5+C \\\\ C & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = t^2-6t + 9.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-6t+9 & =0 \\\\ (t-3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=3 \\,\\textrm s.$" 13194139535402 true] [17592186175849 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347359 13194139535402 true] [17592186175849 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347366 13194139535402 true] [17592186175849 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347373 13194139535402 true] [17592186175849 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347412 13194139535402 true] [17592186175849 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347439 13194139535402 true] [17592186175850 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347380 13194139535402 true] [17592186175850 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347387 13194139535402 true] [17592186175850 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347417 13194139535402 true] [17592186175850 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347422 13194139535402 true] [17592186175850 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347432 13194139535402 true] [17592186175879 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347394 13194139535402 true] [17592186175879 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347401 13194139535402 true] [17592186175879 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347408 13194139535402 true] [17592186175879 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347428 13194139535402 true] [17592186175879 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347446 13194139535402 true] [17592186347359 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347360 13194139535402 true] [17592186347359 1089 "q-120755" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347359 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535402 true] [17592186347359 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ $a(t)=2t-5,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. The positive direction is left to right. If the particle has velocity $v=10\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=6\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the left." 13194139535402 true] [17592186347359 1113 #uuid "2be0b383-f913-54bf-8320-d57af23e10c3" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347359 1145 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t-5)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-5t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(6)=10.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =(6)^{2}-5(6)+C \\\\ 10 & =6+C \\\\ C & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}-5t+4.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}-5t+4=(t-1)(t-4)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/assets/ad76f817f7c6e4a254e8881c34270eb875e72d821531566cb141cb393dcead72.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever $1<t<4.$" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347360 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347361 13194139535402 true] [17592186347360 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347362 13194139535402 true] [17592186347360 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347363 13194139535402 true] [17592186347360 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347364 13194139535402 true] [17592186347360 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347365 13194139535402 true] [17592186347360 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535402 true] [17592186347360 1041 "selection" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347360 1104 #edb/ref 17592186347361 13194139535402 true] [17592186347360 1110 #uuid "5605b0db-6bc6-5bf3-97a7-5406c5e813de" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347361 1034 "1<t<4" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347361 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347361 1111 #uuid "8c7132f7-047a-51cb-82fb-2281c5091c9c" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347362 1034 "0<t<1" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347362 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347362 1111 #uuid "67612c77-0a0f-5f8b-a6bc-9aa5913c6395" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347363 1034 "t>4" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347363 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347363 1111 #uuid "0dcd8556-a6c6-513e-b2be-72186509d693" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347364 1034 "t>1" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347364 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347364 1111 #uuid "cd674330-ae61-565f-bb46-40b875c09a51" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347365 1034 "0<t<4" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347365 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347365 1111 #uuid "5acda7ef-40eb-5924-9298-ae379d4f1f67" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347366 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347367 13194139535402 true] [17592186347366 1089 "q-120757" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347366 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535402 true] [17592186347366 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-6t-4,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  The positive direction is left to right. If the particle is stationary when $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the left." 13194139535402 true] [17592186347366 1113 #uuid "3000b364-333a-5918-a5a9-f4862710ed59" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347366 1145 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-6t-4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(4)^{3}-3(4)^{2}-4(4)+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t=t(t-4)(t+1)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/assets/002f547b882940d087d6ed9d228334ec163931cfea0665eafdfe8b6ec8d863da.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever $0<t<4.$" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347367 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347368 13194139535402 true] [17592186347367 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347369 13194139535402 true] [17592186347367 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347370 13194139535402 true] [17592186347367 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347371 13194139535402 true] [17592186347367 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347372 13194139535402 true] [17592186347367 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535402 true] [17592186347367 1041 "selection" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347367 1104 #edb/ref 17592186347372 13194139535402 true] [17592186347367 1110 #uuid "fd447f17-d0bd-57f8-8c74-29519d271329" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347368 1034 "0<t<3" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347368 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347368 1111 #uuid "171625ef-8dc7-5b34-a8bd-0d3b7b06942c" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347369 1034 "1<t<4" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347369 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347369 1111 #uuid "472a4780-c05a-552d-b4a2-219deabc49d3" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347370 1034 "t>0" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347370 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347370 1111 #uuid "ada0f5b2-db9a-5904-9297-12420b6cb2fe" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347371 1034 "t>4" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347371 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347371 1111 #uuid "c1e2a6e0-73c1-5839-89a3-675aec1875ef" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347372 1034 "0<t<4" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347372 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347372 1111 #uuid "02fc2ff1-db16-5b1a-ad1d-dbe65323d8e0" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347373 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347374 13194139535402 true] [17592186347373 1089 "q-120758" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347373 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535402 true] [17592186347373 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-8t+4,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  The positive direction is left to right. If the particle is stationary when $t=2\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the right." 13194139535402 true] [17592186347373 1113 #uuid "44fe0fb9-e449-5b62-8c42-a1a49cb8c77e" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347373 1145 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-8t+4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(2)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(2)^{3}-4(2)^{2}+4(2)+C \\\\ 0 & =8-16+8+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+4t.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *right*, we must solve the inequality $v>0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+4t=t(t-2)^{2}>0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/assets/9254ea32fc55b2a703e52f55f4292d9708eef63ae147a9f1d2eac40b85eff319.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v>0$ whenever $0<t<2$ or $t>2.$" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347375 13194139535402 true] [17592186347374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347376 13194139535402 true] [17592186347374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347377 13194139535402 true] [17592186347374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347378 13194139535402 true] [17592186347374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347379 13194139535402 true] [17592186347374 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535402 true] [17592186347374 1041 "selection" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347374 1104 #edb/ref 17592186347377 13194139535402 true] [17592186347374 1110 #uuid "1deb23e8-243c-5879-a0bc-5abdd02f8eb6" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347375 1034 "0<t<2" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347375 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347375 1111 #uuid "71f50476-7bb0-52b6-a106-0307c753e044" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347376 1034 "t>2" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347376 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347376 1111 #uuid "8e909557-402f-5a89-af4a-a06569e803c1" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347377 1034 "$0<t<2$ and $t>2$" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347377 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535402 true] [17592186347377 1111 #uuid "5c96c751-5057-56ea-a9ae-fcc675d8458e" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347378 1034 "2<t<3" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347378 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347378 1111 #uuid "0cc2eadc-65a0-5eb6-88d3-80c1b56fb6d6" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347379 1034 "t>1" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347379 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347379 1111 #uuid "123e61bb-87c5-56e9-93a1-2b831ce6387c" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347380 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347381 13194139535402 true] [17592186347380 1089 "q-120784" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347380 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535402 true] [17592186347380 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=15-3t,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=14\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity." 13194139535402 true] [17592186347380 1113 #uuid "3b21641d-ae30-55b8-ba2d-d86f82ac4d9c" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347380 1145 "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 15-3t & =0 \\\\ t & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(15-3t) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(5)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (15-3t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =15t-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=14.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15(1)-\\frac{3}{2}(1)^{2}+C & =14 \\\\ C & =\\frac{3}{2}-1\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{2}+15t-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(5)=\\frac{1}{2}+15(5)-\\frac{3}{2}(5)^{2}=38\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347381 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347382 13194139535402 true] [17592186347381 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347383 13194139535402 true] [17592186347381 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347384 13194139535402 true] [17592186347381 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347385 13194139535402 true] [17592186347381 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347386 13194139535402 true] [17592186347381 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535402 true] [17592186347381 1041 "selection" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347381 1104 #edb/ref 17592186347383 13194139535402 true] [17592186347381 1110 #uuid "7c206aea-ad4e-5055-b51f-e5b9863968ed" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347382 1034 "{v}_{\\text{max}}=37.5\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=5\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347382 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347382 1111 #uuid "afe52287-dbf9-55a5-ad9c-cf9db68b0922" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347383 1034 "{v}_{\\text{max}}=38\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=5\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347383 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347383 1111 #uuid "8a60ca76-c90b-5646-abd4-9991066d6771" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347384 1034 "{v}_{\\text{max}}=14\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=1\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347384 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347384 1111 #uuid "cc94764a-6f49-5af6-b0c6-8c3d48aea0ac" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347385 1034 "{v}_{\\text{max}}=24.5\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=2\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347385 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347385 1111 #uuid "951b86ca-e7a1-547c-9e08-39cdec444d68" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347386 1034 "{v}_{\\text{max}}=75\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=5\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347386 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347386 1111 #uuid "c2cd26d8-2498-5d91-bab0-2d31c640525b" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347387 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347388 13194139535402 true] [17592186347387 1089 "q-120817" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347387 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535402 true] [17592186347387 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=16t-{t}^{3},$ where $t>0$ is the time  in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=44\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=2\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity." 13194139535402 true] [17592186347387 1113 #uuid "abfa67aa-cfad-591f-9920-3a4586b2b99e" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347387 1145 "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 16t-{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ t(16-{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ t(4-t)(4+t) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0$, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(16t-{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =16-3{t}^{2} \\\\ {v}^{″}(4) & =16-3(4)^{2} \\\\  & =-32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(4)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (16t-{t}^{3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(2)=44.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8(2)^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{4}+C & =44 \\\\ 32-4+C & =44\\,⟹\\,C=16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=16+8{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{4}.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(4)=16+8(4)^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}(4)^{4}=80\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347388 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347389 13194139535402 true] [17592186347388 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347390 13194139535402 true] [17592186347388 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347391 13194139535402 true] [17592186347388 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347392 13194139535402 true] [17592186347388 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347393 13194139535402 true] [17592186347388 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535402 true] [17592186347388 1041 "selection" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347388 1104 #edb/ref 17592186347389 13194139535402 true] [17592186347388 1110 #uuid "f833f5a6-2fc9-59e9-8446-89816f02c9f1" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347389 1034 "{v}_{\\text{max}}=80\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=4\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347389 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347389 1111 #uuid "76e0049b-e827-5e62-910c-cde6073e7393" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347390 1034 "{v}_{\\text{max}}=44\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=2\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347390 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347390 1111 #uuid "eb5e6ec6-841d-51dd-bf7e-257f6b844688" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347391 1034 "{v}_{\\text{max}}=22\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=1\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347391 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347391 1111 #uuid "a2ce41a0-d76c-52b5-8dac-80b6d9414ab7" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347392 1034 "{v}_{\\text{max}}=144\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=4\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347392 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347392 1111 #uuid "2e50994c-c2ed-5a2f-84bb-76209c44b40a" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347393 1034 "{v}_{\\text{max}}=64\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=4\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347393 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347393 1111 #uuid "d40cb7a2-d33d-58fa-a3de-7a5d4fcab1e1" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347394 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347395 13194139535402 true] [17592186347394 1089 "q-120843" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347394 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535402 true] [17592186347394 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-6t-4,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=30\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=5\\,\\text{s},$ determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary." 13194139535402 true] [17592186347394 1113 #uuid "80fca8ce-b95f-5f16-bd48-d2a1b1bfcf3f" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347394 1145 "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-6t-4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(5)=30.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30 & =(5)^{3}-3(5)^{2}-4(5)+C \\\\ 30 & =125-75-20+C \\\\ 30 & =30+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t & =0 \\\\ t({t}^{2}-3t-4) & =0 \\\\ t(t-4)(t+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $t=-1,0,4.$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we discard solutions $t=-1,0.$ \nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347395 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347396 13194139535402 true] [17592186347395 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347397 13194139535402 true] [17592186347395 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347398 13194139535402 true] [17592186347395 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347399 13194139535402 true] [17592186347395 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347400 13194139535402 true] [17592186347395 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535402 true] [17592186347395 1041 "selection" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347395 1104 #edb/ref 17592186347396 13194139535402 true] [17592186347395 1110 #uuid "4bd36f75-8321-5120-a843-88471e9add0c" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347396 1034 "t=4\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347396 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347396 1111 #uuid "35bf3d2d-740f-5161-8c08-ba736d3aaf1e" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347397 1034 "t=0\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347397 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347397 1111 #uuid "10ce8023-cdbd-56fa-bf0c-3d08c0dc0889" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347398 1034 "t=1\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347398 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347398 1111 #uuid "b44f0bd7-1386-513f-b009-8619f7c87db3" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347399 1034 "t=5\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347399 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347399 1111 #uuid "e2012a77-0e22-5edb-beca-85ec3a7bbfd6" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347400 1034 "t=2\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347400 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347400 1111 #uuid "3df54e57-8de2-59c4-a19e-81d1c2084c8d" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347401 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347402 13194139535402 true] [17592186347401 1089 "q-120847" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347401 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535402 true] [17592186347401 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\sqrt{t}-t,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s},$ determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary." 13194139535402 true] [17592186347401 1113 #uuid "a8cd1df7-b5ee-52ff-a440-dc872cbe554f" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347401 1145 "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\sqrt{t}-t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{2}{3}(1)^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{1}{6}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}=0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3/2}-3{t}^{2}=0 \\\\ {t}^{3/2}(4-3{t}^{1/2}) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\,\\frac{16}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ the particle is stationary when $t=\\frac{16}{9}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347402 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347403 13194139535402 true] [17592186347402 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347404 13194139535402 true] [17592186347402 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347405 13194139535402 true] [17592186347402 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347406 13194139535402 true] [17592186347402 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347407 13194139535402 true] [17592186347402 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535402 true] [17592186347402 1041 "selection" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347402 1104 #edb/ref 17592186347403 13194139535402 true] [17592186347402 1110 #uuid "5fd3bd4f-c504-5ecc-bdee-da6e6dcdbde4" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347403 1034 "t=\\frac{16}{9}\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347403 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347403 1111 #uuid "5328a585-92b3-5837-a587-e243d0e71855" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347404 1034 "t=\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347404 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347404 1111 #uuid "6aa95437-4e13-5748-8205-4673ec98d39b" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347405 1034 "t=1\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347405 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347405 1111 #uuid "5a8a455a-bb46-5f61-ad45-6f4fb1550d38" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347406 1034 "t=16\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347406 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347406 1111 #uuid "139391db-d583-5c92-8049-d49e4a7df0a1" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347407 1034 "t=\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347407 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347407 1111 #uuid "cda47f23-e809-59f7-9623-50d963c13109" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347408 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347409 13194139535402 true] [17592186347408 1089 "q-349452" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347408 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535402 true] [17592186347408 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=4\\sqrt{t},$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s},$ determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary.\n\nThe particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}$\\text{s}.$" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347408 1113 #uuid "1d16b015-b019-5781-ac85-1dc7c79b07e0" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347408 1145 "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (4\\sqrt{t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{7}{3}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3} & =\\frac{8}{3}(1)^{3/2}+C \\\\ \\frac{7}{3} & =\\frac{8}{3}+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 8{t}^{3/2} & =1 \\\\ {t}^{3/2} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ t & ={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{2/3}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347409 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347410 13194139535402 true] [17592186347409 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347411 13194139535402 true] [17592186347409 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535402 true] [17592186347409 1041 "field-1" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347409 1104 #edb/ref 17592186347410 13194139535402 true] [17592186347409 1110 #uuid "dfee791d-34de-56db-ab0b-52acf7beef16" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347410 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347410 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347410 1111 #uuid "0180f83f-9a51-5d54-82b9-5c40faaaa53c" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347411 1034 "1" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347411 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347411 1111 #uuid "1c0a402f-dd29-5769-a225-d3f7bcc1f3d4" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347412 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347413 13194139535402 true] [17592186347412 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347415 13194139535402 true] [17592186347412 1089 "q-354843" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347412 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535402 true] [17592186347412 1100 "A runner moves along a straight track relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-2t-12,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. The positive direction is left to right. If the runner is stationary when $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time interval in which the runner moves to the left.\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$<t<$\n\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347412 1113 #uuid "74c3dc46-3475-526b-a5f2-58b999ab5666" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347412 1145 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-2t-12)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(4)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(4)^{3}-(4)^{2}-12(4)+C \\\\ 0 & =64-16-48+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the runner at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-12t.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the runner is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-12t=t(t-4)(t+3)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/assets/27b9a41b0ad5c3ff98f6d76e24f0dc74e863d8f63a4c033fc41b7f7f2ebd72ea.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever $0\\,<\\,t\\,<\\,4.$" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347413 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347414 13194139535402 true] [17592186347413 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535402 true] [17592186347413 1041 "field-1" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347413 1104 #edb/ref 17592186347414 13194139535402 true] [17592186347413 1110 #uuid "7d76f71f-f6bb-5c3f-8d9a-c88aadd2f3a9" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347414 1034 "0" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347414 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347414 1111 #uuid "3b252b44-084d-547d-94e2-ffe621d06abd" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347415 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347416 13194139535402 true] [17592186347415 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535402 true] [17592186347415 1041 "field-2" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347415 1104 #edb/ref 17592186347416 13194139535402 true] [17592186347415 1110 #uuid "ab74e609-b28e-540e-8f5c-1dc5141a8fb4" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347416 1034 "4" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347416 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347416 1111 #uuid "c435ff07-035e-5ba5-9b5f-e31c437778ac" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347417 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347418 13194139535402 true] [17592186347417 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347420 13194139535402 true] [17592186347417 1089 "q-354997" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347417 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535402 true] [17592186347417 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=3\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the earliest time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity.\n\nThe maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached first time at $t=$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{s}.$" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347417 1113 #uuid "c42ef4dd-57c4-5ec3-b31b-d457d324859f" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347417 1145 "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$  \n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =0 \\\\ 4t & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer.  \n\nTherefore, the extrema of the velocity function, for $t>0,$ are\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{5\\pi }{8},…\n$$\n\nWe now test the extrema values, starting with the least, using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =-48\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =-48\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =-48\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-48\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-48<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$  Therefore, the first time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity is $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+C & =3 \\\\ 0+C & =3\\,⟹\\,C=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nv(t)=3+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{\\text{max}} & =v(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =3+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =3+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity  ${v}_{\\text{max}}=6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached first time at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347418 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347419 13194139535402 true] [17592186347418 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535402 true] [17592186347418 1041 "field-1" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347418 1104 #edb/ref 17592186347419 13194139535402 true] [17592186347418 1110 #uuid "1a6b04ad-eb88-5f33-a2dd-e7fbe24850cf" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347419 1034 "6" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347419 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347419 1111 #uuid "4761a1cb-26c1-5010-801f-2629c4a25c87" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347420 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347421 13194139535402 true] [17592186347420 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535402 true] [17592186347420 1041 "field-2" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347420 1104 #edb/ref 17592186347421 13194139535402 true] [17592186347420 1110 #uuid "9ebfd94f-bef6-594b-843c-97325e65ce7b" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347421 1034 "\\frac{\\pi}{8}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347421 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347421 1111 #uuid "215af426-de46-5442-8fb0-080aa787c3f0" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347422 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347423 13194139535402 true] [17592186347422 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347426 13194139535402 true] [17592186347422 1089 "q-354998" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347422 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535402 true] [17592186347422 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t),$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the earliest time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity.\n\nThe maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached first time at $t=$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{s}.$" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347422 1113 #uuid "756b3b1d-bd10-5418-b2ff-ec0ca51de06d" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347422 1145 "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$  \n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & =0 \\\\ 5t & =\\pi n \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{5}n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer.  \n\nTherefore, the extrema of the velocity function, for $t>0,$ are\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{5},\\frac{2\\pi }{5},\\frac{3\\pi }{5},…\n$$\n\nWe now test the extrema values, starting with the least, using the second derivative test. For this, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\  & =50\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5},$ the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) & =50\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =50\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-50<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{5})<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$ Therefore, the first time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity is $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+C & =2 \\\\ -2+C & =2 \\\\ C & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nv(t)=4-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{\\text{max}} & =v(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =4-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =4-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity  ${v}_{\\text{max}}=6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached first time at  $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347423 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347424 13194139535402 true] [17592186347423 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347425 13194139535402 true] [17592186347423 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535402 true] [17592186347423 1041 "field-1" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347423 1104 #edb/ref 17592186347424 13194139535402 true] [17592186347423 1110 #uuid "b46fda3f-1819-5d2b-b6c5-4bdc7ea4c1da" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347424 1034 "6" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347424 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347424 1111 #uuid "f6a02a03-3676-5442-8ba9-7ad757ac4388" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347425 1034 "4" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347425 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347425 1111 #uuid "19613a9d-1f0c-5113-8521-c6f055a0cd94" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347426 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347427 13194139535402 true] [17592186347426 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535402 true] [17592186347426 1041 "field-2" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347426 1104 #edb/ref 17592186347427 13194139535402 true] [17592186347426 1110 #uuid "558d7c46-5562-5e0d-b935-3568a185730a" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347427 1034 "\\frac{\\pi}{5}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347427 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347427 1111 #uuid "4a571561-a287-5a82-9ba5-4038d426bfd3" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347428 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347429 13194139535402 true] [17592186347428 1089 "q-355266" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347428 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535402 true] [17592186347428 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\frac{2}{t},$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t={e}^{3}\\,\\text{s}$, determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary. Do not round your answer.\n\nThe particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347428 1113 #uuid "2782961b-f944-50aa-9da6-831addd71b10" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347428 1145 "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v({e}^{3})=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3})+C \\\\ 4 & =6+C \\\\ C & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)-2.$ \n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)-2 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t) & =1 \\\\ t & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=e\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347429 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347430 13194139535402 true] [17592186347429 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347431 13194139535402 true] [17592186347429 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535402 true] [17592186347429 1041 "field-1" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347429 1104 #edb/ref 17592186347430 13194139535402 true] [17592186347429 1110 #uuid "fc076205-34f1-5cb9-af02-01bc649cc56f" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347430 1034 "e" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347430 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347430 1111 #uuid "aecd7b3c-388a-5bc4-bcd2-b9c65bdea044" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347431 1034 "1" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347431 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347431 1111 #uuid "fc1b5410-c540-5e94-acc1-7caf2760304e" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347432 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347433 13194139535402 true] [17592186347432 1089 "q-49950" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347432 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535402 true] [17592186347432 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=2-\\frac{2}{3}t,$ where $t⩾0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity." 13194139535402 true] [17592186347432 1113 #uuid "2cf69b21-8b56-5114-88ce-a8607481a182" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347432 1145 "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 2-\\frac{2}{3}t & =0 \\\\ t & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t)$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-\\frac{2}{3}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(3)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2-\\frac{2}{3}t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2t-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-\\frac{1}{3}(0)^{2}+C & =4 \\\\ 0+0+C & =4\\,⟹\\,C=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2t-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{2}+4.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(3)=2(3)-\\frac{1}{3}(3)^{2}+4=7\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347433 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347434 13194139535402 true] [17592186347433 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347435 13194139535402 true] [17592186347433 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347436 13194139535402 true] [17592186347433 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347437 13194139535402 true] [17592186347433 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347438 13194139535402 true] [17592186347433 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535402 true] [17592186347433 1041 "selection" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347433 1104 #edb/ref 17592186347435 13194139535402 true] [17592186347433 1110 #uuid "0ed0ae30-1508-5f62-a3cf-94feebd8851a" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347434 1034 "{v}_{\\text{max}}=13\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=2\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347434 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347434 1111 #uuid "d4df4282-2d4f-56bd-af03-103f59a7e12f" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347435 1034 "{v}_{\\text{max}}=7\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347435 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347435 1111 #uuid "7711b930-01f2-5a95-bace-cc856fca6b1a" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347436 1034 "{v}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{29}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=1\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347436 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347436 1111 #uuid "82c65a4b-9a32-5b54-98f8-0561623315c9" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347437 1034 "{v}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{20}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=2\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347437 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347437 1111 #uuid "17e09cd5-7896-5ee4-8a9b-c741a377f458" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347438 1034 "{v}_{\\text{max}}=4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347438 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535402 true] [17592186347438 1111 #uuid "30cb3d5b-a778-5aba-8855-acfc1cbdc187" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347439 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347440 13194139535402 true] [17592186347439 1089 "q-81769" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347439 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535402 true] [17592186347439 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=1-2t$, where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. The positive direction is left to right. If the particle has velocity $v=-6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=4\\,\\text{s}$, determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the right." 13194139535402 true] [17592186347439 1113 #uuid "c6b97c2d-852b-5777-8eb7-01af2dbc5e9e" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347439 1145 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (1-2t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t-{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(4)=-6.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =(4)-(4)^{2}+C \\\\ -6 & =4-16+C \\\\ -6 & =-12+C \\\\ C & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=-{t}^{2}+t+6.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *right*, we must solve the inequality $v>0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n-{t}^{2}+t+6=-({t}^{2}-t-6)=-(t+2)(t-3)>0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t),$ we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/assets/74db16dc00f5d08637cef02960409b7fd15c4bd3c19f577fa5a1f30ac8c402d3.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v>0$ whenever $0<t<3.$" 13194139535402 true] [17592186347440 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347441 13194139535402 true] [17592186347440 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347442 13194139535402 true] [17592186347440 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347443 13194139535402 true] [17592186347440 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347444 13194139535402 true] [17592186347440 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347445 13194139535402 true] [17592186347440 1040 